68 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Mountains are among the most enduring forms in our landscape. They appear permanent, immovable, almost outside of time. Yet our relationship with them is constantly changing through maps, regulations, tourism, protected areas, digital records, and human interpretation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
69 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (In this project, I begin with real mountains and real geographical information, but I do not seek to represent them. Instead, I observe what remains after layers of information, memory, and perception are distilled.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
70 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Each painting starts from a specific summit, yet gradually moves away from description. Elevations, boundaries, access rules, place names, and fragments of human language emerge and fade within the surface. The mountain remains present, but no longer as a landscape. It becomes a trace.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
71 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Through reduction rather than addition, Vette explores the distance between a place and our understanding of it. What appears fixed reveals itself as something continuously filtered through observation and interpretation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (As with all my work, the objective is not to explain the world, but to distill a fragment of it.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
